

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Scope set: browser app only ([38;5;153mapp.js[39m).

  Next fork, and it's the one that decides the whole shape: [1mwhere the logs end up.[0m In a browser, [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m in
  production runs on the user's machine and you never see it — so "debug production issues" only works if the records
  leave the page somehow.

  - [1mConsole only (structured)[0m — levels, timestamps, consistent record shape, stays in devtools. Buys: clean, zero
    infrastructure, no privacy surface, works today. Costs: you still can't see a stranger's logs; it only helps when
    you can reproduce it yourself or walk a user through devtools. Cheap to change later — a sink-based logger can gain
    a remote sink without touching call sites.
  - [1mLocal ring buffer + export[0m — console plus the last N records kept in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m/memory, with a way to dump them
    (copy-to-clipboard or a [38;5;153mwindow.__logs()[39m hook). Buys: logs survive a reload and a user can hand you the dump from a
    real failure, with no backend. Costs: a retention/size decision and a redaction pass, since records now persist on
    disk. Reversible.
  - [1mRemote log shipping[0m — batched POST of records to a collector. Buys: real production visibility, unattended. Costs:
    you need an endpoint that exists ([38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is currently a stub, so there's no server here yet), plus consent/PII
    handling, batching, retry, and failure-when-offline behavior. This is the hard-to-reverse one: it creates an
    interface and starts sending user data off-device.

  My recommendation: [1mlocal ring buffer + export[0m, built on a small logger core with pluggable sinks. It gives you
  something genuinely useful for production reports without inventing a backend that doesn't exist, and the sink seam
  means adding remote shipping later is a new sink, not a rewrite of every call site.
